function COMPONENT(request : dval) {
    print("<div class=\"doc-detail-layout\"><main class=\"content doc-page doc-detail\"><h2>7. Arrays, tuples, and structs</h2><div class=\"doc-section content\"><p>Use an array for an ordered collection of one type. Use a tuple for a short fixed group. Use a struct when fields need names.</p><h2>Arrays</h2><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [&quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;Grace&quot;]\n    print(names[0], &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p><strong>Output</strong></p><pre><code class=\"language-text\">Ada\n</code></pre><p>Array indexes start at zero. An invalid index traps.</p><p>Declare the item type when an array starts empty:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names : [string] = []\n    names.push(&quot;Ada&quot;)\n    print(names[0], &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p><code>push</code>, <code>pop</code>, <code>insert</code>, <code>remove</code>, <code>clear</code>, <code>reserve</code>, and <code>resize</code> change an array.</p><h2>Array values</h2><p>An array is a mutable reference value inside one workspace:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">function append_three(values : [s32]) {\n    values.push(3)\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var first := [1, 2]\n    var second := first\n    append_three(second)\n    print(first[2], &quot; &quot;, second[2], &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p>Assignment and parameter passing share the array identity. Returns and captures also share it. Content mutation through a parameter affects the caller. A parameter binding remains immutable, so <code>values = [3]</code> is invalid. Array growth preserves identity.</p><p>A <code>for</code> loop reads array items in order:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [&quot;Ada&quo")
    print("t;, &quot;Grace&quot;]\n    for name, index := names {\n        print(index, &quot;: &quot;, name, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n    }\n}\n</code></pre><p>The optional index follows the value and has type <code>s32</code>. The loop rechecks the current array length. It safely stops after shrinkage and can visit appended items. The loop retains each current managed item while the body runs.</p><p>The loop item is a value, not an assignable array position. Use an index to replace an item.</p><h2>Tuples</h2><p>A tuple is immutable. It has fixed positions and can contain different types:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var result := (200, &quot;ok&quot;)\n    print(result[0], &quot; &quot;, result[1], &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p><code>(value)</code> only groups an expression. It does not create a one-item tuple.</p><p>A tuple can spread into matching function parameters:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">function add(left : s32, right : s32) s32 { -&gt; left + right }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var pair := (20, 22)\n    var answer := add(...pair)\n    print(answer, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><h2>Structs</h2><p>A struct defines a named fixed layout:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.x, &quot; &quot;, point.y, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p>The generated constructor takes fields in declaration order. A struct instance is a mutable reference value inside one workspace. Assignment, parameters, returns, and captures share its identity. Struct fields are assignable:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction move_right(point : Point) {\n    point.x = point.x + 1\n}\n\nfunc")
    print("tion RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    move_right(point)\n    print(point.x, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p>The field mutation affects the caller. The parameter binding itself remains immutable.</p><p>A receiver-first function can use method syntax:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction total(point : Point) s32 { -&gt; point.x + point.y }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.total(), &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</code></pre><p>This is the same call as <code>total(point)</code>.</p><h2>Choose the right shape</h2><p>Use an array when every item has one static type. Use a tuple for a small fixed return value. Use a struct for application data with a stable shape. Use a <code>dval</code> map or list for decoded input and data that crosses a Bearer boundary.</p></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [&quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;Grace&quot;]\n    print(names[0], &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div><p><strong>Output</strong></p><div class=\"example-output\"><div class=\"example-output-label\">Output</div><pre>Ada\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names : [string] = []\n    names.push(&quot;Ada&quot;)\n    print(names[0], &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">function append_three(values : [s32]) {\n    values.push(3)\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var first := [1, 2]\n   ")
    print(" var second := first\n    append_three(second)\n    print(first[2], &quot; &quot;, second[2], &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [&quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;Grace&quot;]\n    for name, index := names {\n        print(index, &quot;: &quot;, name, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n    }\n}\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var result := (200, &quot;ok&quot;)\n    print(result[0], &quot; &quot;, result[1], &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">function add(left : s32, right : s32) s32 { -&gt; left + right }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var pair := (20, 22)\n    var answer := add(...pair)\n    print(answer, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.x, &quot; &quot;, point.y, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction move_right(point : Point) {\n    point.x = point.x + 1\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    move_right(point)\n    print(point.x, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example")
    print("</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction total(point : Point) s32 { -&gt; point.x + point.y }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.total(), &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div><nav class=\"guide-navigation\" aria-label=\"Capy guide navigation\"><div class=\"guide-previous\">Previous: <a href=\"/doc/\">Guide index</a></div><div class=\"guide-index\"><a href=\"/doc/\">Guide index</a></div><div class=\"guide-next\">Next: <a href=\"/doc/\">Guide index</a></div></nav></main><aside class=\"doc-section see-also\"><h3>See also</h3><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/values-and-types/\">3. Types</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/functions-and-closures/\">5. Functions</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/guide/dynamic-values/\">8. Dynamic values</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/length/\">length</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/set/\">set</a></aside></div>")
}
